[4/92] GPQA Diamond/Physics Imagine an uncharged spherical conductor of radius $R$ having a
We have a spherical conductor with a cavity. The charge +q inside the cavity will induce opposite charges on the inner cavity surface and an equal +q on the outer surface of the conductor. For a point outside the conductor, the field is as if all charge +q were at the center of the sphere (since the conductor is spherical and the induced outer charge distributes uniformly). Therefore, the field depends only on the distance L from the sphere's center, not on the cavity location.

Thus the magnitude is  
\[
E = \frac{1}{4\pi\epsilon_0} \frac{q}{L^2}.
\]

