[29/92] SuperGPQA/Engineering On a certain satellite channel, the sender transmits frames with
We need to compute throughput for different window sizes.  
Given:  
- Frame length = 512 bytes = 4096 bits  
- Data rate = 64 kb/s  
- One-way propagation delay = 270 ms  
- Acknowledgment frame is very short (negligible transmission time).  

First, transmission time for one frame:  
\( t_{tx} = \frac{4096 \text{ bits}}{64{,}000 \text{ b/s}} = 0.064 \text{ s} = 64 \text{ ms} \)  

Round-trip propagation delay = \( 2 \times 270 = 540 \text{ ms} \)  

Total time per frame without pipelining (window=1):  
\( T = t_{tx} + 2 \times \text{propagation} = 64 + 540 = 604 \text{ ms} \)  

Throughput for window=1:  
\( \frac{4096}{0.604} \approx 6781.5 \text{ b/s} \approx 6.78 \text{ kb/s} \)  

For window size \(W\), throughput = \( \frac{W \times 4096}{64 \text{ ms} + 540 \text{ ms}} \) if \(W\) is small enough that sender is not always busy. But if window is large enough to keep sender busy continuously, throughput = 64 kb/s.  

The maximum number of frames that can be in flight during one round trip:  
\( \frac{540}{64} = 8.4375 \), so window > 8 can saturate.  

For window=7:  
Throughput = \( \frac{7 \times 4096}{0.604} \approx 47470 \text{ b/s} \approx 47.5 \text{ kb/s} \)  

For window=17:  
Since 17 > 8.4375, throughput = 64 kb/s.  

For window=117:  
Also > 8.4375, throughput = 64 kb/s.  

Thus values:  
Window 1: ~6.8 kb/s  
Window 7: ~47.5 kb/s  
Window 17: 64 kb/s  
Window 117: 64 kb/s  

This matches option C.  

Answer: C
