[61/92] GPQA Diamond/Chemistry The Michael reaction is a chemical process in organic chemistry
We need to determine the Michael addition products.

**First reaction:**  
Methyl 2-oxocyclohexane-1-carboxylate is a β-keto ester. Under basic conditions (NaOEt), it forms an enolate. The electrophile is 2,4-dimethyl-1-(vinylsulfinyl)benzene, which has a vinyl group attached to a sulfinyl (S=O) group. The enolate adds to the terminal carbon of the vinyl group, forming a new C–C bond at the α-position (carbon 1 of the cyclohexane ring). The product is methyl 1-(2-((2,4-dimethylphenyl)sulfinyl)ethyl)-2-oxocyclohexane-1-carboxylate.

**Second reaction:**  
Ethyl 2-ethylbutanoate is an ester with α-hydrogens. NaH deprotonates to form an enolate. The electrophile is methyl 2-cyclopentylidene-2-phenylacetate, an α,β-unsaturated ester. The enolate adds to the β-carbon of the unsaturated ester, giving a new bond at the α-carbon of the ester enolate. The product is ethyl 2-ethyl-2-(1-(2-methoxy-2-oxo-1-phenylethyl)cyclopentyl)butanoate.

Comparing with choices, that matches option C.

**Final answer:**  
Answer: C
